// CLOSED 2026-09-24 (squeue #93): a fresh rvalue source of a `let` array pattern is a statement temporary — the unbound elements drop at the end of the let.
// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row let_array_pattern_temp_source_remainder_late_run (tier 1, run 1). WHEN THE
// SOURCE OF A `let` ARRAY PATTERN IS A TEMPORARY, THE UNBOUND ELEMENTS ARE DESTROYED TOO LATE.
//
// `let [_, y] = mk(p);` where `mk` RETURNS `[D; 2]`: rustc destroys the unbound element 0 (1) at the
// END OF THE LET STATEMENT, because the call result is a statement-scoped temporary, and y (2) only
// at scope exit — n = 12, exit 0 (MEASURED rustc 1.98.1). TODAY n = 21: the unbound element is
// destroyed at scope exit instead, AFTER the binding.
// ⚠ Logos ALREADY HAS THIS RULE WRITTEN DOWN and does not apply it here: docs/spec/statements.md
// `stmt.scope.temp-drop-at-stmt-end` — "fresh owned (droppable) temporaries materialized while
// lowering a statement live to the end of that statement and have their destructors run there".
// The spill temp that now owns the unbound elements is exactly such a temporary. So this is a
// conformance defect against Logos own spec, not a divergence question.
// Count is CORRECT (each element destroyed once); only the timing is wrong, so no count-based or
// rc-based oracle sees it. Opened by round 2026-09-16j-arrpath2, which fixed the LEAK this shape
// had on the base binary (n=2, two elements lost) and declined the timing (reason 3, unpriced).
package let_array_pattern_temp_source_remainder_late_run;
extern fn printf(fmt: *const u8, ...) -> i32;
struct D { v: i64, c: *mut i64 }
impl Drop for D { fn drop(self: &mut D) { unsafe { *self.c = *self.c * 10i64 + self.v; } } }
struct W { a: D, b: D }
fn mk(p: *mut i64) -> [D; 2] { return [D { v: 1i64, c: p }, D { v: 2i64, c: p }]; }
fn g(p: *mut i64) -> i64 {
    let [_, y] = mk(p);
    return y.v;
}
fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut n: i64 = 0i64;
    let p: *mut i64 = &mut n;
    let k: i64 = g(p);
    let got: i64 = unsafe { n };
    unsafe { printf("k=%ld n=%ld\n".as_ptr(), k, got); }
    if k != 2i64 { return 2i32; }
    if got != 12i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
